{
  "summary": "This study investigates how dual twisted interfaces in CVD-grown trilayer MoS2 affect second harmonic generation (SHG). By fabricating nine distinct stacking configurations, the researchers analyzed Raman and photoluminescence data to identify stacking-dependent phonon and excitonic responses. Atomic-resolution techniques confirmed dual twisted interfaces and a supermoiré lattice. They developed a framework linking interface geometry to SHG intensity and polarization, demonstrating TT-MoS2 as a tunable nonlinear optical platform.",
  "key_findings": [
    "CVD-grown trilayer MoS2 with dual twisted interfaces exhibits stacking-dependent phonon and excitonic responses",
    "Atomic-resolution techniques confirmed dual twisted interfaces and a supermoiré lattice structure",
    "A framework linking interface geometry to SHG intensity and polarization was established for tuning nonlinear optical responses"
  ],
  "study_type": "CVD-grown twisted trilayer MoS2 configurations",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a foundational understanding of multi-interface twisted coupling in van der Waals materials, enabling precise engineering of nonlinear optical responses through structural design."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses residual tumor persistence after high-frequency irreversible electroporation (H-FIRE) for prostate cancer. It identifies DNA damage repair as the key mechanism allowing reversible survival of residual cells. By engineering a piezoelectric nanomaterial (BOIS), the researchers exploit H-FIRE-induced membrane permeability to deliver BOIS into residual cells. Ultrasound activation triggers BOIS to generate an electric field and reactive oxygen species (ROS), causing irreversible damage through dual mechanisms. This approach significantly enhances tumor cell eradication by converting reversible injury to irreversible death.",
  "key_findings": [
    "H-FIRE-induced membrane permeability enables intracellular delivery of piezoelectric nanomaterial BOIS",
    "Ultrasound-activated BOIS generates electric fields and ROS to induce dual damage mechanisms",
    "The strategy converts residual tumor cells from reversible to irreversible injury, improving eradication"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a self-reinforcing therapeutic loop where focal ablation delivers nanomaterials to enhance residual tumor destruction, offering a novel mechanism for improving focal therapy outcomes."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates blood-brain barrier protein transport mechanisms using bioorthogonal proteomics. It identifies 191 plasma proteins sampled and 92 transported into the brain, enriched for carrier functions. The findings reveal cell-type specific protein tropism, with APOA1 targeting astrocytes via SR-B1 and ABCA1, a key Alzheimer's risk gene. Early Alzheimer's models show selective transport remodeling, and hepatic APOA1 overexpression rescues deficits.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The healthy blood-brain barrier samples 191 plasma proteins and permits 92 into brain parenchyma",
    "APOA1 crosses the BBB via SR-B1 and engages astrocytic ABCA1, a key Alzheimer's risk gene",
    "Early Alzheimer's models exhibit reduced APOA1 and increased SERPINA1 uptake, with hepatic APOA1 overexpression rescuing synaptic and behavioral deficits"
  ],
  "study_type": "Bioorthogonal proteomics",
  "scientific_significance": "This work redefines the blood-brain barrier as a selective interface that recruits neuroprotective circulatory proteins to maintain brain health, providing mechanistic insights into early Alzheimer's disease pathogenesis."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how PD-L1 expression in germinal centers influences humoral immunity. Using integrated analyses of secondary lymphoid organs, researchers found B cell zone reticular cells express PD-L1, which promotes GC formation and autoantibody tolerance loss. Mechanistically, PD-L1 deletion enhanced TFH cell Sostdc1 production and TFR differentiation, destabilizing T-B interactions. Patient tumor biopsies linked a TFH SOSTDC1 signature to immunotherapy responsiveness.",
  "key_findings": [
    "B cell zone reticular cells express PD-L1 in germinal centers",
    "PD-L1 deletion increases TFH Sostdc1 production and TFR differentiation",
    "A tumor-infiltrating TFH SOSTDC1 signature inversely correlates with immunotherapy responsiveness"
  ],
  "study_type": "Integrated analyses of secondary lymphoid organs",
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies a non-canonical immunogenic role for PD-L1 in germinal centers, demonstrating how it regulates T-B collaboration to promote humoral immunity and potentially enhances immunotherapy outcomes."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of observing distributed cortical circuits during natural behavior using a novel wireless mesoscope, Airscope. It achieves 4 μm lateral resolution over a 6 mm field of view at 12.5 Hz, weighing 1 gram. The device enables high-throughput calcium imaging of >8,500 neurons across multiple animals in diverse behavioral contexts, including social dominance, maze exploration, and fear conditioning. It identifies cortical activity patterns correlating with social dominance and competition outcomes, and demonstrates spinal-cortical Granger causality during fear conditioning.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Airscope enables wireless, high-resolution cortical imaging of >8,500 neurons per animal during natural behaviors",
    "Cortical activity patterns correlate with social dominance and competition outcomes between paired animals",
    "Spinal-cortical Granger causality is observed during fear conditioning"
  ],
  "study_type": "Wireless mesoscope",
  "scientific_significance": "This technology bridges large-scale cortical recording with unconstrained natural behavior, enabling previously impractical multi-animal studies of neural circuits in complex behavioral contexts."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated closed-loop control of spinal cord blood flow post-spinal cord injury using real-time ultrasound measurements and norepinephrine infusion. The approach involved designing a feedback system that adjusts vasopressor delivery based on dynamic flow data, validated through simulations, rodent trials, and pig models. Results demonstrated reduced blood flow variability and improved functional recovery compared to standard dosing.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Closed-loop control using ultrasound-guided norepineph:phrine infusion regulated spinal cord blood flow",
    "The system reduced blood flow deviations and variability versus constant dosing",
    "Functional recovery was supported in rodent survival models with the controller"
  ],
  "study_type": "Closed-loop control system validated in simulations and animal models",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a clinically translatable method for precise spinal cord perfusion management, advancing the ability to tailor therapeutic interventions to injured tissue without relying on indirect hemodynamic measurements."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of achieving coordinated CRISPR-based transactivation in vivo by engineering a compact Cas1.2m variant (denCas12m). The approach involves leveraging its self-processing activity to enable multiplexed gene activation from a single AAV vector. Results show efficient activation of multiple endogenous genes in mouse models, including phenotypic rescue in Duchenne muscular dystrophy and Scn1a haploinsufficiency, with up to six genes activated per vector.",
  "key_findings": [
    "denCas12m enables self-processing of multi-gRNA arrays into functional guides",
    "Single AAV vector activates six distinct endogenous genes in mice",
    "Phenotypic rescue achieved in Duchenne muscular dystrophy and Scn1a haploinsufficiency models"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo mouse models",
  "scientific_significance": "This work advances CRISPR-based multiplexed gene activation by demonstrating a compact, self-processing Cas12m variant that overcomes AAV vector size limitations for simultaneous regulation of multiple genes in vivo."
}

{
  "summary": "This study identifies human serine protease inhibitor A3 (SERPINA3) and its mouse ortholog SERPINA3k as soluble SIRPα ligands that suppress immune cell activity. The research demonstrates SERPINA3(k) alleviates inflammatory conditions like pneumonia and autoimmune diseases, while a mutant variant retains immunosuppressive function without adverse effects. Cancer cells secrete high levels of SERPINA3(k), suggesting its role in tumor immunity and potential therapeutic targeting.",
  "key_findings": [
    "SERPINA3 and SERPINA3k function as soluble SIRPα ligands suppressing immune cell activity",
    "SERPINA3(k) treatment reduces inflammation in pneumonia and autoimmune models",
    "A SERPINA3D2 mutant eliminates adverse effects while maintaining immunosuppressive capacity"
  ],
  "study_type": "Preclinical models",
  "scientific_significance": "This discovery reveals a potent endogenous SIRPα ligand with dual roles in modulating inflammation and antitumor immunity, providing a mechanistic basis for novel immunotherapeutic strategies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates ultrathin BiFeO3 multiferroics to address degradation of magnetic and polar order in thin films. Using first-principles calculations, symmetry analysis, and atomic imaging, researchers identified a phase transition at four unit cells that eliminates magnetic dead layers. The ultrathin film exhibits collinear monoclinic structure with d-wave altermagnetic order, enabling stable multiferroic behavior at device-relevant thicknesses.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Ultrathin BiFeO3 films at four unit cells show no magnetic dead layers",
    "Collinear monoclinic phase with d-wave altermagnetic order stabilizes at this thickness",
    "Short-circuit electrostatic boundary conditions and epitaxial strain drive the phase transition"
  ],
  "study_type": "First-principles calculations, symmetry analysis, atomic-resolution imaging and magnetic imaging",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a pathway to stabilize unconventional multiferroicity at device-relevant thicknesses, overcoming traditional scaling limits for oxide electronics."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the thermodynamic origin of phonon-mediated thermal Hall effects in honeycomb antiferromagnets. Researchers measured longitudinal and transverse thermal conductivities in nickel phosphorus trisulfide (NiPS3), finding peak values at the same temperature with a thermal Hall angle within a theoretical bound. The transverse conductivity strongly depends on b-axis magnetization but shows no correlation with phonon mean free path, suggesting an intrinsic mechanism.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Longitudinal and trans: thermal conductivities peak at the same temperature",
    "Thermal Hall angle at peak temperature respects a theoretical bound",
    "Transverse thermal conductivity correlates strongly with b-axis magnetization but not with phonon mean free path"
  ],
  "study_type": "Experimental",
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies an intrinsic mechanism for phonon thermal Hall effects in honeycomb antiferromagnets, advancing understanding of thermal transport in magnetically ordered materials."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether immune cells directly transduce mechanical cues into nociceptive signals during ulcerative colitis. Using a minimally inflammatory zymosan model, researchers identified Piezo1 in CX3CR1+ macrophages as a mechanosensitive channel that triggers NGF synthesis via calcium influx and NF-κB nuclear translocation. This macrophage-derived NGF sensitizes TRPV1+ nociceptors, establishing a direct mechanosensory relay independent of cytokine cascades. Genetic ablation of Piezo1, Ngf, or Ntrk1 abolished visceral hyperalgesia in colitis models.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Piezo1 in CX3CR1+ macrophages directly transduces mechanical cues into nociceptive signals during colitis",
    "Piezo1 activation induces NGF synthesis through calcium influx and NF-κB nuclear translocation",
    "Genetic deletion of Piezo1, Ngf, or Ntrk1 eliminates visceral hyperalgesia in colitis models"
  ],
  "study_type": "Minimally inflammatory intracolonic zymosan model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies tissue-resident macrophages as autonomous mechanical sensors that establish a direct, parallel pain transmission pathway to sensory nerves during inflammatory bowel disease."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates closed nanopores in silicon-carbon anodes as stress-regulation units to mitigate lithiation-induced deformation and interphase degradation. By engineering heteroatom-doped carbon hosts with abundant closed pores, the research redirects expansion stress inward, suppresses electrode swelling, and enhances Li+ kinetics. The nitrogen-doped composite achieves 1756.5 mAh g-1 capacity, 90.2% initial efficiency, and 868.4 mAh g-1 at 3 C, with reduced swelling to 13.1% after 50 cycles. This scalable design enables practical high-energy anodes for lithium-ion batteries.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Closed nanopores redirect expansion stress inward to suppress electrode swelling",
    "Heteroatom doping enhances electronic conductivity and Li+ insertion/extraction kinetics",
    "Nitrogen-doped silicon-carbon composite achieves 1756.5 mAh g-1 capacity with 868.4 mAh g-1 at 3 C"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro engineering of heteroatom-doped carbon hosts",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes closed-pore stress regulation as a scalable structural strategy for practical low-expansion silicon-carbon anodes, advancing high-energy lithium-ion battery technology."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated inter-individual variation in human striatum using single-nucleus RNA sequencing across 178 donors. It found common patterns of cell abundance and gene expression, with astrocytes, microglia, and OPCs correlating across brain regions. OPCs declined 40% with aging, and age could be predicted from gene expression with 6.6-year error. Genetic variants influenced nearly 10,000 genes cell-type specifically.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Astrocytes, microglia, and oligodendrocyte precursor cells correlated across gray-matter regions",
    "Oligodendrocyte precursor cell proportions decreased 40% from age 30 to 80",
    "Donor age was predicted from RNA expression with a median absolute error of 6.6 years"
  ],
  "study_type": "single-nucleus RNA sequencing",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a comprehensive atlas of cell-type-specific gene expression variation across human brain regions, providing a foundation for understanding neurodevelopmental and age-related vulnerabilities."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated how a PD-L1×4-1BB bispecific antibody exerts antitumor effects via T cell cross-priming by PD-L1+ type 1 conventional dendritic cells (cDC1s). The mechanism was assessed through in vivo tumor models and cDC1 depletion experiments. Results showed that PD-L1×4-1BB efficacy depends on cDC1-T cell interactions, cross-presentation, IL-12, 4-1BB costimulation, and CD40L-mediated cDC1 licensing, leading to enhanced CD8+ T cell proliferation and tumor control.",
  "key_findings": [
    "PD-L1×4-1BB efficacy is independent of tumor PD-L1 expression but requires cDC1s for antitumor activity",
    "cDC1-specific PD-L1 deletion impaired tumor control, indicating critical role of PD-L1+ cDC1s",
    "PD-L1×4-1BB enhanced CD8+ T cell proliferation via cross-presentation, IL-12, 4-1BB signaling, and cDC1 licensing"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo tumor models with cDC1 depletion experiments",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes cDC1-T cell cross-priming as a pivotal mechanism for bispecific antibody efficacy, highlighting the convergence of dendritic cell licensing and costimulatory signals in antitumor immunity."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the functional architecture of the human insula using intracranial electrical stimulation and electroencephalography in 87 participants. It identifies four distinct regions: a dorsal-posterior region processing nociceptive/thermoceptive signals, a ventral-posterior region integrating somatic information, a mid-anterior region linked to visceral sensations and anxiety, and an anterior-polar region involved in salience detection. The anterior region shows strong connections with the prefrontal cortex, exerting direct influence over other insular regions, suggesting a pathway for cognitive modulation of pain and bodily perception.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The insula has four distinct functional regions: dorsal-posterior for nociceptive/thermoceptive signals, ventral-posterior for somatic integration, mid-anterior for visceral sensations and anxiety, and anterior-polar for salience detection.",
    "The anterior-polar region exhibits strong connections with the prefrontal cortex, directly influencing other insular regions.",
    "This architecture links cytoarchitecture to asymmetric electrophysiological interactions, providing mechanistic insights into cognitive modulation of pain and interoception."
  ],
  "study_type": "Intracranial electrical stimulation and electroencephalography",
  "scientific_significance": "This work defines a functional architecture of the human insula that connects cytoarchitecture to directed electrophysiological interactions, advancing understanding of how cognitive processes modulate pain and bodily perception."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of endoscopic gastrointestinal fistula repair by developing a thermoresponsive hydrogel sealant. The Janus-adhesive hydrogel, composed of poly(N-isopropylacrylamide) and Ga3+, enables single-step tissue adhesion with biocompatibility, water/acid resistance, active shrinkage, and antibacterial properties. It effectively repairs fistulas in animal models, showing potential for broader clinical applications.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The hydrogel exhibits Janus-type adusing to tissues with single-step formation",
    "It demonstrates biocompatibility, water/acid resistance, active shrinkage, and antibacterial properties",
    "The sealant successfully repairs gastrointestinal fistulas in rat and pig models"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo animal models",
  "scientific_significance": "This hydrogel provides a minimally invasive solution for gastrointestinal fistula repair, advancing the field of endoscopic sealants with integrated functional properties for clinical translation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated an mRNA-lipid nanoparticle vaccine's efficacy against H5N.1 influenza in lactating dairy cows. Using two intramuscular doses followed by intramammary viral challenge, vaccinated cows showed significantly reduced milk viral RNA and mastitis incidence compared to controls. The vaccine induced HA-specific antibodies in serum and milk, demonstrating protection against H5N1 replication in mammary tissue.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Vaccinated cows exhibited ~1,000-fold lower peak viral RNA in milk post-challenge",
    "Vaccination reduced mastitis and mammary fibrosis incidence compared to controls",
    "The vaccine elicited HA-reactive antibodies in both serum and milk"
  ],
  "study_type": "Randomized controlled trial with two intramuscular doses and intramammary challenge",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides evidence that mRNA-LNP vaccines can effectively protect dairy cows from H5N1 influenza replication in mammary tissue, addressing a critical gap in livestock disease control strategies."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines cytokine pathways in CNS homeostasis and disease, focusing on key circuits involving CSF-1, IL-34, TGFβ, IL-33, IL-1, TNF, GM-CSF, IFNγ, and interferons. It outlines how these cytokines coordinate glial-neuronal interactions, immune responses, and tissue repair mechanisms, with miscalibration potentially contributing to neurological disorders.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Cytokines regulate CNS integrity through glial-neuronal communication and immune cell coordination",
    "Specific pathways like CSF-1/IL-34 maintain homeostasis, while inflammatory cytokines drive tissue damage responses",
    "Cytokine dysregulation can disrupt CNS stability, leading to neurological disease across multiple contexts"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a framework for understanding cytokine networks that preserve, destabilize, and restore CNS integrity, highlighting mechanisms underlying neurological disease pathogenesis."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of synthesizing free-standing two-dimensional metal oxides at the single-atomic-layer limit. Using electron-beam-induced dehalogenation of layered metal oxyhalides, researchers fabricated CrO, ErO, and BiO monolayers exceeding 104 nm². Density functional theory confirmed thermodynamic and kinetic stability of monolayer CrO, predicting antiferromagnetic order below 250 K. The method enables heterostructures and excitonic studies in ultrathin metal oxides.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Controlled fabrication of free-standing CrO, ErO, and BiO monolayers via electron-beam dehalogenation",
    "Monolayer CrO exhibits antiferromagnetic order below 230 K (predicted by DFT)",
    "Fabrication of metal oxide heterostructures with pronounced excitonic responses"
  ],
  "study_type": "Electron-beam-induced dehalogenation of layered metal oxyhalides",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a scalable method for synthesizing single-atomic-layer metal oxides, enabling exploration of quantum phenomena and excitonic effects in ultrathin two-dimensional materials."
}

{
  "summary": "This study developed a wireless electrocatalytic closed-loop device (ECHO) using platinum-decorated gold nanoclusters in graphite felt to selectively oxidize blood glucose to gluconate. In diabetic mice, low-power wireless activation restored glucose homeostasis within an hour, reduced body weight and triglycerides, and shifted metabolism toward lipid utilization without adverse effects over six weeks. The system enabled drug-free, autonomous glucose management via smartphone control.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Wireless activation restored glucose homeostasis within 1 hour in diabetic mice",
    "Reduced body weight and triglycerides while shifting metabolism to lipid utilization",
    "Enabled drug-free, autonomous closed-loop management via smartphone-controlled continuous glucose monitoring"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo mouse model",
  "scientific_significance": "This approach demonstrates a non-pharmacological method for managing type 2 diabetes and obesity through direct glucose conversion, offering a potential pathway for drug-free metabolic regulation without systemic side effects."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how nuclear NAD+ regulates innate immune memory through epigenetic mechanisms. It demonstrates that trained immunity increases nuclear N. AD+ levels, which inhibit KDM5A-mediated histone demethylation, maintaining H3K4me3 at inflammatory gene promoters. This sustains enhanced responsiveness to restimulation. NAMPT inhibition or deletion impaired trained immunity and antitumor responses, while NMN supplementation rescued these effects in aged mice and human cancer models.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Elevated nuclear NAD+ during trained immunity inhibits KDM5A demethylase activity",
    "Sustained H3K4me3 levels at inflammatory gene promoters enhance memory responsiveness",
    "NAMPT deficiency or inhibition impairs trained immunity and antitumor activity"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo studies",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes nuclear NAD+ as a critical epigenetic regulator that links metabolic changes to chromatin accessibility in innate immune memory formation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates biodegradable capacitive-coupling bioelectronics (CCB) for wireless electrical stimulation and longitudinal electrophysiological monitoring during peripheral nerve regeneration. The CCB platform integrates biodegradable molybden. electrodes with wireless stimulation and compound nerve action potential recording. In rat sciatic nerve defects, CCB enhanced Schwann cell activity, axonal regeneration, and motor recovery, matching autograft outcomes while enabling non-invasive monitoring without repeated tissue exposure.",
  "key_findings": [
    "CCB enabled wireless electrical stimulation and longitudinal electrophysiological monitoring in the same animal without repeated nerve exposure",
    "CCB improved Schwann cell activation, axonal regeneration, remyelination, and motor recovery in rat sciatic nerve defects",
    "CCB outcomes approached autograft repair levels with coordinated neurovascular remodeling and pro-regenerative immune modulation"
  ],
  "study_type": "Rat sciatic nerve defect model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work advances peripheral nerve repair by transitioning from passive conductive scaffolds to intelligent, biodegradable bioelectronics that integrate therapeutic stimulation and dynamic functional monitoring."
}

{
  "summary": "This study integrated single-cell epigenomic data from 2.5 million adult mouse brain nuclei to construct a comprehensive atlas. It combined four histone modifications, transcriptomes, chromatin accessibility, DNA methylation, and 3D genome organization to define 100+ cell subclasses and chromatin states across 81% of the genome. Active states marked by combinatorial histone modifications better predict functional enhancers than chromatin accessibility alone, while H3K27me3 and H3K9me3 define repressive states. The resource enables deep learning models for predicting epigenomic features and gene expression from DNA sequence.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Integration of four histone modifications, transcriptomes, chromatin accessibility, DNA methylation, and 3D genome organization defined 100+ brain cell subclasses",
    "Combinatorial histone modifications better predict functional enhancers than chromatin accessibility alone",
    "H3K27me3 and H3K9me3 mark distinct repressive chromatin states for cell-type-specific gene silencing"
  ],
  "study_type": "Single-cell epigenomic profiling",
  "scientific_significance": "This atlas provides a unified framework for annotating the regulatory genome of the adult mouse brain, enabling the prediction of cell-type-specific epigenomic features and gene expression from DNA sequence."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated how vascular endothelial growth factor receptor-1 (VEGFR-1) signaling maintains intestinal T follicular helper (Tfh) cell stemness to control pathobiont expansion and obesity. Using Flt1-deficient T cells in mice, researchers found impaired Tfh fitness, reduced secretory IgA, and unchecked growth of Taurinivorans. This led to taurine depletion, suppressed adipocyte lipolysis, and obesity. The VEGFR-1-AKT pathway limited p53-mediated senescence, preserving Tfh stemness. Therapeutic IgA administration reduced Taurinivorans and alleviated obesity, demonstrating a Tfh-microbiota-obesity axis with clinical relevance.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Flt1 deletion in T cells caused spontaneous obesity in aging mice",
    "VEGFR-1+ Tfh cells in Peyer's patches and mesenteric lymph nodes were essential for gut microbial homeostasis",
    "Taurinivorans overgrowth depleted host taurine, driving obesity through suppressed adipocyte thermogenesis"
  ],
  "study_type": "Mouse model with human validation",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a VEGFR-1-AKT-mediated Tfh stemness mechanism that regulates gut microbiota composition and metabolic health, providing a target for obesity interventions."
}
